[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/ring_buffer/lib/ring_buffer_impl.py: The pop implementation leaves an uncoverable contract-violation branch: the empty-buffer guard body that raises BufferEmptyError (the statement inside the if self.is_empty condition at lines 100-103) is a caller-precondition violation that the low-level implementation specification grounds as requires(not self.is_empty, BufferEmptyError). Conforming tests can never violate that precondition, so 100% statement coverage is unattainable while that raise remains a plain executable statement; this is why the author's # CHANGE, which added an explicit empty-guard raise, did not resolve the coverage deficit. Restructure the code so the empty case is not an executable branch (express it as the contract's caller precondition), or, if the guard must remain for defense, mark it unreachable via 'from pretense import unreachable; unreachable()', or annotate it with '# pragma: no cover (assumption: precondition enforced by caller)'.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/ring_buffer/lib/ring_buffer_impl.py: Resolve coverage feedback: the empty-buffer pop guard raises BufferEmptyError for the caller precondition requires(not self.is_empty, BufferEmptyError), which conforming tests never violate. Kept the defensive guard for contract conformance and annotated the raise with '# pragma: no cover (assumption: precondition enforced by caller)' so the uncoverable branch no longer blocks 100% coverage; normal pop path remains fully covered.
